PRACTICAL NO: 3
TITLE: INTERFACING TM1637 4-DIGIT 7-SEGMENT DISPLAY WITH RASPBERRY PI 4

AIM:
To interface a TM1637 4-digit 7-segment display module with Raspberry Pi 4, understand the two-wire serial communication protocol, and display a real-time digital clock using Python.

COMPONENTS REQUIRED:
1. Raspberry Pi 4 Model B
2. TM1637 4-Digit 7-Segment Display Module (with colon)
3. Female-to-Female Jumper Wires
4. 5V Power Supply for Raspberry Pi
5. Active Internet connection (for system time synchronization via NTP)

PIN CONNECTIONS:
TM1637 Pin VCC connects to 5V (Physical Pin 2)
TM1637 Pin GND connects to Ground / GND (Physical Pin 6)
TM1637 Pin CLK connects to GPIO 23 (Physical Pin 16)
TM1637 Pin DIO connects to GPIO 24 (Physical Pin 18)

THEORY:

1. TM1637 Driver IC Architecture:
The TM1637 is an integrated LED drive control circuit with a keyboard scan interface. In 4-digit display modules, it eliminates the need to control all 28 individual segment pins ($4 \text{ digits} \times 7 \text{ segments} + \text{colons}$) directly from the microcontroller. Instead, it drives common-anode LED segments via an internal multiplexing and display RAM circuitry, requiring only two communication lines: Clock (CLK) and Data Input/Output (DIO).

2. Communication Protocol (Two-Wire Serial Interface):
Although the interface resembles I2C, TM1637 does not strictly follow the standard I2C address protocol (it lacks device addressing bytes).
- CLK (Clock Line): Synchronizes data transfer. Data on the DIO line must remain stable during the clock's HIGH period and can only change state when CLK is LOW.
- DIO (Data Line): Serial bidirectional data transfer line.
- Start Condition: DIO transitions from HIGH to LOW while CLK is HIGH.
- Stop Condition: DIO transitions from LOW to HIGH while CLK is HIGH.
- Acknowledge (ACK): The chip pulls DIO LOW on the 9th clock pulse to confirm receipt of each 8-bit packet.

3. Time Representation and Display Multiplexing:
The 4-digit display consists of four digits arranged sequentially (Digit 0 to Digit 3). The Python library converts numeric integer values into 7-segment lookup bitmasks (segments a, b, c, d, e, f, g, and DP/colon). The internal oscillator of the TM1637 refreshes the LED segments dynamically at high frequency, giving the illusion of a continuous, flicker-free display to the human eye through persistence of vision.

4. Virtual Environment (venv) on Raspberry Pi OS:
Modern Raspberry Pi OS releases enforce PEP 668, preventing global installation of Python packages via pip (`pip install`) into the system environment. Creating a dedicated virtual environment (`python3 -m venv env`) isolates project dependencies safely.

